title: Basic Services

files:
  - em.prog:em.examples.basic/Ex02_BlinkerP.em.ts
  - rpi.2040:rpi.distro.2040/BoardC.em.ts

steps:
  - cmds:
      - open 1
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;Back to Basics}

      We return now to {[cu]BlinkerP} &ndash; to see how {[cu]BoardC} bridges portable **EMS** code with a distro's HW-specific content.

      We've already visited {[cu]BoardC} &ndash; in a **Language Overview** {[tr,100/03/09]} as well as when touring **Basic Examples** {[tr,101/01/05]}.

      The next tours in this group approach {[cu]BoardC} differently, layering functionality to support more complex examples:

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&nbsp; **Basic Services** &mdash; {[cu]DebugP}, {[cu]FiberP}, {[cu]HelloP}<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&nbsp; **Timers&thinsp;+&thinsp;Interrupts** &mdash; {[cu]OneShot[1-3]P}<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&nbsp; **Sleep&thinsp;+&thinsp;Wakeup** &mdash; {[cu]Alarm[1-2]P}, {[cu]TickerP}<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]}&nbsp; **Edge Interrupts** &mdash; {[cu]Button[1-3]P}<br>

      <div class="em-info">

      Support for **CoreMark** [&thinsp;{[tr,102/01/01]}, {[tr,102/07/03]}&thinsp;] will also fall into place as we incrementally expand {[cu]BoardC}.

      </div>

  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - fold 2 3 4
      - fold 2 6 8
      - fold 2 6 8
      - fold 2 12 14
      - fold 2 15 16
      - fold 2 17 24
      - fold 2 25 28
      - fold 2 55 88
      - mark 2 A 5
      - mark 2 B 29
      - mark 2 C 39
      - mark 2 D 55
    focus: [2, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% &ndash; Import&thinsp;&middot;&thinsp;Export}

      While supplied by the `rpi.2040` distro in this case, all {[cu]BoardC} composites follow a common design pattern:

      {[cu]BoardC} will typically {[ck]import}{[bm,A]} a large number of units &ndash; many portable [{[cu]LedT}], some distro-supplied [{[cu]GpioT}].

      &nbsp;💡&thinsp;Use **F12** to navigate to any of these imported files

      {[cu]BoardC} will also {[ck]export}{[bm,B]} named delegates [&thinsp;{[cu]AppLed}&thinsp;] created by cloning **EMS** templates.

      {[cu]BoardC} then defines a set of default values {[bm,C]} applied when reading {[bm,D]} the distro's special `em-boards` file.

      <div class="em-info">

      So now, let's see how all of these pieces come together within the {[cu]BoardC} {[cs]em$configure} meta-function.

      </div>



  - cmds:
      - open 2
      - fold 2 3 4
      - fold 2 6 8
      - fold 2 6 8
      - fold 2 12 14
      - fold 2 15 16
      - fold 2 17 24
      - fold 2 25 28
      - fold 2 57 61
      - fold 2 69 71
      - fold 2 72 74
      - fold 2 83 85
      - fold 2 87 88
      - mark 2 A 5
      - mark 2 B 29
      - mark 2 C 39
      - mark 2 D 55
      - mark 2 E 56
      - mark 2 F 64
      - mark 2 G 65
    focus: [2, 60]
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;%[cu]BoardC% &ndash; Configuration}

      A {[cu]BoardC}{[cs]em$configure} function has four key tasks:

      {[BM,D]} fetch info on the current {[em]Board} from `em-boards`<br>
      {[BM,E]} force inclusion of core modules at build-time<br>
      {[BM,F]} bind compatible delegates to module proxies<br>
      {[BM,G]} bind values to module config parameters<br>

      Among the portable units seen here, {[cu]Common} stands apart by using "empty" delegates for _unbound_ proxies {[tr,100/03/08]}.

      <div class="em-info">

      Adding services via {[cu]BoardC} over the next several tours will in fact require binding more {[cu]Common} proxies.

      </div>

      Take a peek at {[cu]BoardController}, which invokes several {[cu]Common} proxies during program startup and shutdown.

      &nbsp;💡&thinsp;Select any proxy or config name, and then hit **F12**.

  - text: |
